# 4. CORE GAMEPLAY MECHANICS

### 4.1 The Conveyor Belt System

This is the central gameplay mechanic.

Unlike traditional endless-running games, the character does not run forward through the environment.

Instead:

- The character runs continuously in approximately the same position.
- The conveyor belt moves underneath the character.
- Obstacles are positioned along the conveyor.
- The moving belt carries those obstacles towards the character.
- The character must avoid incoming obstacles.

The conveyor belt is long, straight and linear.

It is NOT a circular treadmill, hamster wheel or roller coaster.

It should look like an industrial conveyor belt with a continuous moving surface.

### 4.2 Conveyor Belt Layout

The conveyor has three visual lanes:

- Left.
- Middle.
- Right.

However, the character is not locked into these lanes.

Movement is free and smooth within the boundaries of the conveyor belt.

The player can move between positions rather than snapping between fixed lanes.

### 4.3 Player Controls

The game uses intuitive touchscreen swipe controls.

| Gesture | Action |
|---|---|
| Swipe left | Move towards the left |
| Swipe right | Move towards the right |
| Swipe up | Jump |
| Swipe down | Duck / slide |
| Diagonal swipe | Move diagonally |

Movement must be responsive and smooth.

The character must remain within the conveyor belt's boundaries.

### 4.4 Obstacle Collision

Obstacles must have clearly defined collision areas.

A collision occurs when the character makes contact with an obstacle without successfully avoiding it.

The initial design proposal is:

**One collision ends the run.**

This makes survival challenging and gives every successful dodge importance.

This rule can be adjusted during playtesting.

